WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.560
We have been hearing it for years.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.140
Climate change is happening.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.230
What I am about to present to you is fact.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:17.360
These are reliable measurements with multiple
peer reviewed papers confirming the information.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.340
Atmospheric carbon dioxide levels are the
highest they have been in over 400 thousand

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:26.200
years, confirmed by our analysis of hundreds
of samples of arctic ice core, tree cores

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.310
and isotope ratios in fossils.

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:33.380
[1] Average global temperatures have risen
by 0.8 degrees celsius since the industrial

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:37.670
revolution began, with two third of that change
occurring since 1975.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.790
[2] The evidence is overwhelming.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:40.800
This is fact.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.980
You are wrong if you deny it.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.170
If these trends continue, and I really shouldn’t
say if because they will continue, we are

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.510
going to continue seeing stronger storms,
more heat waves and droughts, sea levels will

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:56.020
continue to rise even after the ice caps have
vanished in the summer months in about 30

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:00.100
years, and to really make you motivated to
care about this, the world’s economy will

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.100
suffer.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.930
We have been making strides in the technology
required to reduce our emissions of carbon

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:08.120
dioxide, but the change over is happening
too slowly.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:12.610
The most effective thing our world has done
in the past 10 years in battling carbon emissions

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:14.840
was going through a global financial crisis.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:18.300
Countries, like my own, are continually missing
carbon targets.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:24.240
We are going face up to 600 million euro in
fines every year after 2020 until we fulfil

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.090
our promise of reducing our carbon emissions
by 20%.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:31.980
That money is going to come out of our pockets
through carbon taxes.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.950
Maybe then we will start to take climate change
seriously.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.970
We aren’t making significant decreases in
carbon dioxide emissions, we have only really

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.890
leveled out [3] which is not good enough.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:45.580
So, if we aren’t making a difference by
reducing emissions, maybe we can reverse climate

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:46.660
change.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:51.030
Maybe we can engineer our climate, and there
has been multiple suggested methods of doing

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.030
that.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:56.549
In this new video series I’m going to explain
how several geoengineering methods intend

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:59.560
to work, and their potential impact on the
world’s climate.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.680
The first plan we will examine is Afforestation.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.010
Afforestation is pretty much self explanatory.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:08.729
Plant forests, allow them to grow and store
carbon in their wood.

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:12.940
The problem we run into is finding large enough
spaces to plant forests, that would have a

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:17.300
significant impact on the climate and that
would not negatively affect the economies

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.720
of countries employing the method.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:24.170
Taking land that could be used for agriculture
just isn’t a realistic solution, no-one

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:25.710
is going to agree to it.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:29.620
Our options are limited, but we happen to
have huge expanses of land on earth that are

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:33.000
not being used for anything productive, deserts.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.569
Now I know what you are thinking, deserts
are not the best place for growing anything,

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:41.580
but with water desalination technology rapidly
advancing this isn’t as far fetched as it

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:42.580
may seem.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:46.780
We are going to examine the feasibility and
effect of afforestation in the two largest

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:51.200
subtropical deserts in the world, the Sahara
and the Australian outback.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.640
These are the perfect candidates for afforestation,
neither have large competing human populations,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.320
agricultural activity, or large natural animal
and plant populations.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:05.680
Conveniently, they are also in the sub-tropical
zones where a 12 month growth cycle is possible,

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.489
maximising our carbon capture potential.

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:12.000
To maximise our potential further, we need
to pick a suitable tree.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.739
The tree we chose will need to be suited to
this climate, be ever-green, grow rapidly,

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:19.110
and be useful as a commercial resource.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:22.310
The Australian Eucalyptus grandis will be
candidate for this study.

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:26.860
[4] Which also comes with the added benefit
of being a habitat for these cute little shits.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:30.569
Before even bothering to worry about how this
would be done, let’s first see if it’s

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:31.569
worth being done.

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:34.129
Let’s first look at the Sahara as an example.

00:03:34.129 --> 00:03:39.150
Ultimately we are trying to sequester atmospheric
carbon dioxide by storing it in wood.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.940
Every 10,000 square metres could hold about
1 thousand trees, and taking this patch of

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:48.710
the Sahara between the 16 degree and 50 degree
longitudes we have about 9800 billion square

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:54.610
metres of land, ignoring land needed for infrastructure,
that’s about 980 billion trees.

00:03:54.610 --> 00:04:00.071
Planting a forest of this size would increase
the world population of trees by about 33%.

00:04:00.071 --> 00:04:01.550
That’s a lot of trees.

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:05.310
Estimates show [4 ] that this would capture
between 6 and 12 gigatonnes of carbon per

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:09.879
year for about a century, before it would
meet a steady state where growth would slow

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:13.450
and carbon dioxide in would equal carbon dioxide
out.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:20.930
6 to 12 gigatonnes would capture between 16.3%
to 32.6% of our emissions per year, with humans

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:26.389
generating a total of 36.8 gigatonnes of carbon
dioxide in 2017 [3].

00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:30.150
Ideally we wouldn’t just let the trees grow
and forget about them, we would systematically

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:34.630
cut them down and use them for construction,
synthetic feed-stock or convert them to liquid

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:39.360
biomass fuel to replace our dwindling fossil
fuel supplies and burn that fuel in a power

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:44.360
plant with its own carbon capture technology,
which would reduce emissions further, and

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:47.030
produce new economies for these desert regions.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:52.330
Australia’s desert is about 60% the size
of the Sahara and so we could add an additional

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:57.800
60% to that figure, to bring our best case
scenario to just over 50% capture of our emissions

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.070
per year.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:03.500
Bringing our emission levels per year down
to levels equivalent to the 70s.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:09.030
On the surface this seems great, but what
effect would this actually have on our environment.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:13.330
There are multiple things we need to consider,
first of all is the irrigation itself.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:18.900
A managed forest of this nature would need
about 500 mm per year of water, which equates

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:23.790
to 4900 billion (4.9 x 10^12) metres cubed
of water per year for this number of trees.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:27.889
[4] Where is all this water going to come
from and at what cost?

00:05:27.889 --> 00:05:32.120
Fresh water supplies are obviously rare in
the Sahara, but surprisingly not as rare as

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:33.750
the Australian outback.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:37.870
The world’s largest groundwater aquifer
actually resides beneath the Sahara, and is

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:41.810
shared by four countries Egypt, Libya, Sudan
and Chad.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:46.540
[5] And it is not alone, new studies show
the Sahara is sitting on vast reservoirs of

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:47.650
groundwater.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:51.920
This groundwater supply is vital for many
African countries, with it often being the

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:55.050
primary source of freshwater for their populations.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:06:00.740
Draining at an industrial scale like this
comes with ethical concerns, as it is a non-renewable

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:01.740
resource.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.820
Even these vast reservoirs of water would
run dry within a few years when pumped on

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:06.820
this scale.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:11.810
However, the cost of desalination of sea water
has dropped dramatically in recent years [6],

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:16.340
thanks in large part to countries like Israel,
The United Arab Emirates and Saudi Arabia

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:20.820
who have invested in the technology and all
get over 50% of their drinking water from

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.699
desalination plants.

00:06:22.699 --> 00:06:27.660
This technology still requires energy and
energy comes with a cost, both monetarily

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:29.289
and as a source of carbon dioxide.

00:06:29.289 --> 00:06:35.380
It requires approximately 1.5 kiloWatt hours
of energy to desalinate a metre cubed of water.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:38.570
We then need to pump the water to a height
for distribution.

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:44.270
With the average elevation of the Sahara at
450 metres, this would require a further 2.5

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:48.580
kilowatt hours per metre cubed, bringing our
total energy consumption to 4 kilowatt hours

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:50.479
per metre cubed of water supplied.

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:51.479
[4]

00:06:51.479 --> 00:06:55.250
The cost of this in terms of carbon footprint
and actual cost will vary with the energy

00:06:55.250 --> 00:07:00.340
source used, but considering the location
and nature of the project a mix of solar power

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:05.340
and biomass energy with carbon capture technology
attached to it’s exhaust should be used.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:10.290
Let’s focus on a purely solar powered process
for now, as biomass is more expensive and

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:14.590
has a larger carbon footprint without carbon
capture technology, though it would become

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:19.460
cheaper as the project matures thanks to the
cheap source of fuel on its doorstep.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:24.090
Solar energy costs about 10 cent per kilowatt
hour with a median carbon footprint of 72

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:25.840
grams per kWh. [7]

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:31.080
Putting all this together, the total energy
needed to irrigate this forest with 4900 billion

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:38.000
metres cubed of water will be 19600 terawatt
hours a year, at a cost of 1.96 billion dollars

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:42.370
a year and a carbon footprint of 1.4 gigatonnes
of carbon a year.

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:46.810
Ignoring infrastructure costs, which would
likely push the initial costs into the trillions.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:51.900
This puts our total carbon capture for the
Sahara at a best case scenario of 10.6 gigatonnes

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:57.120
a year at a cost of 184 dollars per tonne
of carbon dioxide captured.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.310
Expecting poor African nations to fund this
alone is unrealistic, so it would be reasonable

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:05.720
to expect countries to pay for this project
through carbon taxes, like those that will

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:10.770
be placed on Ireland in 2020, and thus allowing
them to offset their own carbon emissions

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.199
with funding to the project.

00:08:12.199 --> 00:08:17.990
A litre of petrol when burned emits approximately
2.6 kilograms of carbon dioxide.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:22.830
Thus placing a carbon tax of about 48 cent
per litre of petrol could pay for the project,

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:27.560
if we sell 4 billion litres of petrol with
the added carbon tax, which is about the total

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.750
petrol and diesel consumption of a small country
like Ireland.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:31.750
[8]

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:35.690
So it’s possible at a high cost, but if
the project can stop climate change, maybe

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:36.690
it’s worth it.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:38.870
That’s the next problem we need to address.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:42.619
What effect will the forest actually have
on the world’s environment.

00:08:42.619 --> 00:08:46.170
With the help of climate models we can start
to get a clearer picture of what all this

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:48.610
money and effort would give us.

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:51.089
Temperature being the top of our list of concerns.

00:08:51.089 --> 00:08:54.929
Local temperatures would be affected most
due to the evaporative cooling caused by the

00:08:54.929 --> 00:09:00.249
increase in soil moisture, this would seed
clouds and increase local precipitation substantially,

00:09:00.249 --> 00:09:04.879
allowing us to reduce our ongoing costs, with
heavier irrigation only needed in drier months

00:09:04.879 --> 00:09:06.189
from May to October.

00:09:06.189 --> 00:09:11.499
[8] Local evaporative cooling does not decrease
overall global temperatures, as it just transports

00:09:11.499 --> 00:09:13.649
the heat within earth's atmosphere.

00:09:13.649 --> 00:09:17.949
Critically we want reduce the amount of heat
retained in earth’s atmosphere by reducing

00:09:17.949 --> 00:09:22.019
greenhouse gases, allowing heat to escape
the system entirely.

00:09:22.019 --> 00:09:25.690
One of the biggest concerns with a project
like this is the modification of Earth’s

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:26.690
albedo.

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:29.480
Albedo is a measure how reflective a planet
is.

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:34.529
A higher albedo means we reflect more sunlight
back into space rather than absorbing the

00:09:34.529 --> 00:09:37.709
solar radiation, and thus increasing the temperature.

00:09:37.709 --> 00:09:42.860
Forests have a very low albedo, they are literally
designed to absorb solar radiation.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:48.050
Where as snow and ice have a very high albedo,
they reflect quite a lot of light, as does

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:49.050
sand.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:53.980
Placing forests over regions where sand once
resided will reduce the world’s albedo,

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:58.660
alone it will actually negate the heat lost
due through reduction in greenhouse gases.

00:09:58.660 --> 00:09:59.660
[9]

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:03.829
In this climate model however the clouds seeded
from the forest helps to counteract that decrease

00:10:03.829 --> 00:10:04.829
in albedo.

00:10:04.829 --> 00:10:09.619
The study shows an overall decrease in surface
temperatures, but a significant increase in

00:10:09.619 --> 00:10:11.929
ocean temperatures surrounding the forests.

00:10:11.929 --> 00:10:16.430
The conclusion of the primary paper used for
research in this video is fairly ambiguous

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:20.869
with no definite answer to whether the project
would have a net negative or net positive

00:10:20.869 --> 00:10:22.839
effect on global temperatures.

00:10:22.839 --> 00:10:27.860
while other papers that did not factor the
increase in cloud cover affecting albedo suggesting

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:32.549
that afforestation in the Sahara and Australian
outback would increase global temperatures

00:10:32.549 --> 00:10:39.180
by 0.12 degrees celsius by 2100, compared
a control model where no afforestation occured.

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:40.180
[7]

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:43.939
We also need to worry about the decrease in
fertilization that would occur due to Sahara

00:10:43.939 --> 00:10:48.709
dust no longer being transported to the Amazon
and the Atlantic ocean, which would likely

00:10:48.709 --> 00:10:53.600
decrease plant and plankton growth, and thus
negate much of the carbon sequestration that

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:55.620
this forest would provide.

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:59.939
The desalination plants would also need to
carefully manage their output of highly concentrated

00:10:59.939 --> 00:11:05.179
salt water, as dumping undiluted salt water
into the ocean would wreak havoc on the local

00:11:05.179 --> 00:11:06.399
aquatic environment.

00:11:06.399 --> 00:11:10.910
Overall, I think the idea is an interesting
thought experiment, but practically shows

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:15.260
little evidence of benefit for the labour
and cost needed, and could potentially open

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:18.170
a pandora’s box of unforeseen consequence.

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:23.720
Ultimately our best tool for combating climate
change will be to decrease our carbon emissions,

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:28.470
and that solution is staring us in the face
through the cheap solar and wind energy.

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:32.019
We need more people acting on this issue,
we need more people funding and researching

00:11:32.019 --> 00:11:34.139
alternative energy sources.

